Try this to get a feel for the distances. Picture a car driving at 100 kilometres an hour without ever stopping. To reach the Moon, it would need about 160 days, roughly half a school year.

To reach the Sun, the same car would need about 170 years. Driving to Proxima Centauri, the nearest star after the Sun, would take around 45 million years. This kind of sum is simple: distance divided by [[@speed-distance|speed]]. The hard part is believing the answers.

{{story:big-numbers}}

The common tailorbird, a tiny green bird found in Indian gardens, sews. It pokes holes along the edges of one or two big leaves with its beak, then pulls spider silk or plant fibres through to stitch them into a cone. Inside, it builds a soft cup. In Hindi it is called *darzi*, which means tailor. Rudyard Kipling made one famous in "Rikki-Tikki-Tavi".

Swallows and martins are plasterers. They collect tiny beakfuls of wet mud and stick them together under a ledge or bridge, layer by layer, like bricks. A single nest can take well over a thousand trips.

{{story:spiders-silk}}

Plesiosaurs swam by beating four flippers, a bit like sea turtles or [[@penguin-flight|penguins]] "flying" underwater. One kind, Elasmosaurus, had a neck with over 70 bones. A swan has about 25, and a giraffe has only 7, the same as you. Nobody is quite sure how plesiosaurs used such necks, but they may have helped them sneak up on shoals of fish without being noticed.

The first nearly complete plesiosaur skeleton was found in 1823 at Lyme Regis in England by [[@mary-anning|Mary Anning]]. Years earlier, she and her brother had also dug up one of the first ichthyosaur skulls ever found. She had little schooling, but she became one of the most skilled fossil hunters of her time.

{{story:mary-anning}}

India first tried to get Asiatic cheetahs from Iran, but Iran had too few to share. Today only a handful survive there, in remote deserts and hills, and they are among the rarest big cats on Earth. So India turned to Africa, home to most of the world's cheetahs.

On 17 September 2022, eight cheetahs from Namibia landed in Madhya Pradesh in a specially fitted cargo plane. They were moved to Kuno National Park, where they lived first in fenced enclosures, then were released into the open forest and grassland. Twelve more arrived from [[@south-africa|South Africa]] in February 2023. A month later, the first cubs were born on Indian soil in over seventy years.

{{story:fastest-animal}}

Asiatic lions are the same [[species|species]] as African lions, and are closely related to the lions of West and Central Africa. But they look a little different. Males usually have shorter, thinner manes, so their ears stay visible. Most Asiatic lions have a long fold of skin running along the belly, which African lions rarely show.

Male and female lions in Gir mostly live apart. The females live in small family groups and raise cubs together, while the males form small gangs and join the females mainly to mate. They hunt chital, sambar, nilgai and wild boar, and sometimes cattle.

{{story:ashoka}}

Wright trained in [[@chicago|Chicago]], working for the architect Louis Sullivan, who designed some of America's early [[@skyscrapers|skyscrapers]]. Then he set up his own office and began designing homes for families in Chicago's suburbs.

These became known as Prairie houses. They are low and wide, like the flat grasslands of the American Midwest. Rows of windows run in long bands, roofs reach far out past the walls, and a big fireplace sits at the centre of the home. Inside, Wright often designed everything: the furniture, lamps, rugs and even the stained-glass windows.

{{story:lego}}

The habit of making do goes right back to the start. In 1963, India launched its first rocket from Thumba, a fishing village near Thiruvananthapuram. Scientists used a church as an office. Rocket parts were carried to the launch pad on bicycles, a scene that became a famous photograph.

{{story:space-rocket-bicycle}}

The man behind it, [[@vikram-sarabhai|Vikram Sarabhai]], believed space should help ordinary people: weather forecasts for farmers, television for villages, maps for planners. That purpose shaped how ISRO spends money even today.

A firefighter's gear protects against heat, smoke and falling objects. It includes a thick, fire-resistant suit, a helmet, boots, gloves and a breathing set with an air tank on the back. All together, the gear can weigh around 20 kg, about as much as a big bag of rice, and firefighters have to climb stairs and carry people while wearing it.

Smoke is often more dangerous than flames, because it is hot and poisonous and makes it impossible to see. Firefighters crawl low through smoky rooms, keeping one hand on the wall so they don't get lost, and use thermal cameras to find people.

{{story:fire-burns}}

Designers rarely start with a computer. They sketch ideas in notebooks, cut out cardboard pieces or borrow dice from [[@board-game-design|board games]]. A rough prototype on a table can show in an afternoon whether an idea is fun.

When the idea works, the team builds a simple digital version, often with plain grey boxes instead of characters. Most use a [[@game-engines|game engine]], a ready-made toolkit that handles things like physics, lighting and sound. That lets designers try ideas without writing every line of code from scratch.

{{story:game-engines}}

Satoshi Tajiri grew up in Machida, a suburb of Tokyo, in the 1970s. He spent hours in fields and ponds catching [[@insects-importance|insects]], studying them and comparing them with friends. As the suburb grew, many of those fields were built over.

Tajiri later became a video game fan and started a game magazine, which grew into a studio called Game Freak. He wanted city children to feel the thrill he had felt as a collector. When he saw the Game Boy's link cable, he imagined creatures crawling along it from one player to another. It took his small team about six years to finish the first games.

{{story:insects-importance}}

On 20 July 1968, the first International Special Olympics Summer Games were held at Soldier Field, a big football stadium in Chicago. About 1,000 athletes from the USA and Canada took part.

From there the idea spread fast. Winter games began in 1977. Today Special Olympics says it reaches nearly 4 million athletes and Unified partners in 177 countries and [[territory|territories]], as of 2024. World Games, summer and winter, are held every few years in different countries.

{{story:brain-works}}

Over two hundred years ago, a doctor called Franz Mesmer became famous in [[@paris|Paris]]. He claimed an invisible force he called "animal magnetism" could cure illness. People fainted and shook in his sessions.

The French king set up a commission to check, and one member was the American scientist Benjamin Franklin. The team blindfolded people. Some were told they were being "magnetised" when they weren't, and they still reacted. Others were secretly magnetised and felt nothing. The commission decided the effects came from imagination. It was one of the first blind tests in history, and blind tests are still the backbone of medical science.

{{story:statistics-lies}}

The squeezed data is chopped into packets and sent across the [[@internet-how|internet]]. A call from Bengaluru to a cousin in Chicago may travel through Wi-Fi, mobile towers and data centres, then along [[@undersea-internet|cables on the ocean floor]]. Light in those glass fibres covers that distance in roughly a tenth of a second.

Packets sometimes arrive late, out of order, or not at all. For a video call, waiting for a lost packet is worse than skipping it. So the app guesses the missing bit and moves on. A frozen face or a robot-sounding word is often a lost packet being covered up.

{{story:undersea-internet}}

Over about eleven years she made around 13 trips into Maryland. She brought out her parents, brothers and many others. She used disguises, sang songs with hidden messages, and moved in winter, when nights were long. Helpers, Black and white, hid people in barns, cellars and attics. In Wilmington, Delaware, a Quaker shopkeeper named Thomas Garrett gave her shoes, money and shelter many times. In Philadelphia, William Still recorded the stories of hundreds of people who passed through, which is one way historians know what happened.

She followed rivers and, on clear nights, the [[@north-star|North Star]]. After 1850, a new law meant escaped people could be captured even in northern states, so she guided some of them all the way to [[@canada|Canada]].

{{story:north-star}}

Sati was not practised everywhere in India, but in parts of Bengal in the early 1800s, hundreds of cases were recorded each year. Many widows were pressured into it by relatives. Ram Mohan Roy wrote pamphlets showing that the scriptures did not demand it, and he visited cremation grounds to persuade families to stop.

His work helped convince the Governor-General, Lord William Bentinck, who banned sati in Bengal on 4 December 1829. Many orthodox Hindus were angry and formed a group to fight the ban. They sent an appeal to London, and Roy sailed to England to argue against it. The ban stayed.

{{story:newspapers}}

In 1967, the linguist Eric Lenneberg proposed a "critical period" for language, a window in childhood after which learning a first language becomes much harder. A few rare, sad cases of children who grew up without hearing any language seemed to support the idea, although those children faced many other problems too.

For second languages, the picture is fuzzier. In 2018, researchers from MIT and Harvard gave an online English grammar quiz to about 670,000 people. Their results suggested that the ability to learn grammar stays strong until about 17 or 18 and then slowly declines. To reach native-like grammar, though, it seemed to help to start before about age 10. Other scientists argue that how much time you spend, and how you learn, matter as much as age.

{{story:parrots-talk}}

Plays in Shakespeare's time were working scripts for actors, not books for readers. About half of his plays were never printed while he was alive.

In 1623, seven years after his death, two of his fellow actors, John Heminges and Henry Condell, gathered 36 plays into one large volume, now called the First Folio. Without it, plays such as Macbeth, Twelfth Night and The Tempest would probably have been lost. Around 235 copies are known to survive today, and they are among the most valuable books in the world. It shows how much the [[@printing-press|printing press]] mattered for keeping words alive.

{{story:theatre}}

Too much sugar feeds the [[bacteria|bacteria]] that cause [[@sugar-teeth|tooth decay]], and over many years a diet heavy in sugary drinks is linked to weight gain and type 2 diabetes. Too much salt is linked to high blood pressure later in life.

{{story:sugar-teeth}}

The World Health Organization suggests keeping added sugars below 10% of daily energy, and adults eating less than 5 g of salt a day. One packet of instant noodles with its seasoning can carry a large share of that salt.
